{
  "sn47.46:0.1": "Linked Discourses 47.46 ",
  "sn47.46:0.2": "5. Freedom From Death ",
  "sn47.46:0.3": "Restraint in the Monastic Code ",
  "sn47.46:1.1": "Then a mendicant went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: ",
  "sn47.46:2.1": "“Sir, may the Buddha please teach me Dhamma in brief. When I’ve heard it, I’ll live alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute.” ",
  "sn47.46:2.2": "“Well then, mendicant, you should purify the starting point of skillful qualities. ",
  "sn47.46:2.3": "What is the starting point of skillful qualities? ",
  "sn47.46:2.4": "Live restrained in the monastic code, conducting yourself well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, keep the rules you’ve undertaken. ",
  "sn47.46:2.5": "When you’ve done this, you should develop the four kinds of mindfulness meditation, depending on and grounded on ethics. ",
  "sn47.46:3.1": "What four? ",
  "sn47.46:3.2": "Meditate observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. ",
  "sn47.46:3.3": "Meditate observing an aspect of feelings … ",
  "sn47.46:3.4": "mind … ",
  "sn47.46:3.5": "principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. ",
  "sn47.46:3.6": "When you develop the four kinds of mindfulness meditation in this way, depending on and grounded on ethics, you can expect growth, not decline, in skillful qualities, whether by day or by night.” ",
  "s